.\" Manual page for tacctl, the management CLI for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS).
.\" Render locally with: groff -man -Tascii man/tacctl.1 | less
.TH TACCTL 1 "2026-10-02" "tacctl 0.1.15" "tacctl Manual"
.SH NAME
tacctl \- manage users, groups and scopes for TACACS+ (tacquito) and RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tacctl
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tacctl
is a shell-based management CLI for network-device AAA. It keeps users,
groups, scopes (named CIDR + shared-secret bundles) and connection filters
in a store of its own,
.IR /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ,
and renders the configuration of every enabled
.I backend
from it: TACACS+ (RFC 8907) served by
.BR tacquito ,
installed by
.BR "tacctl install" ,
and, opt-in, RADIUS served by a tacctl-owned FreeRADIUS instance
.RB ( "tacctl backend enable radius" ).
It installs and upgrades the servers, inspects logs and backups, enrolls
Linux hosts (pam_tacplus or pam_radius_auth), and generates ready-to-paste
device configuration for Cisco IOS/IOS-XE and Juniper Junos, plus a
menu-by-menu setup procedure for WTI console servers, for either protocol.
See
.BR BACKENDS ,
.B "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION"
and
.BR "UPGRADING TO 0.1.15" .
.PP
Most commands require root.
.B tacctl
re-executes itself under
.BR sudo (8)
automatically when invoked by a non-root user.
.B tacctl hash
and
.B tacctl version
are deliberate exceptions: they run as the invoking user.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS System lifecycle
.TP
.BI "install [--branch " name "]"
Install tacctl and the TACACS+ backend. Installs the Go toolchain, builds
the tacquito binary, creates the
.B tacquito
service user and the state directory
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
seeds the store (built-in groups and users, the scope
.B lab
with RFC 1918 prefixes and a generated shared secret, printed once),
renders
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
from it, installs the systemd units, and enables the service. An existing
store, or an existing
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
is kept instead (the latter goes through the upgrade's store gate). RADIUS
is not installed: see
.BR "backend enable" .
The optional
.BI --branch " name"
flag selects a specific tacctl repository branch for the install.
.IP
Four built-in users are seeded so fresh installs ship with usable
identities (without writing unknown credentials) and with a sink for
Junos internal accounting:
.B engineer
.RB ( superuser ),
.B operator
.RB ( operator ),
.B viewer
.RB ( readonly ),
and
.B root
.RB ( readonly ).
.IP
The first three carry a placeholder hash that never authenticates;
activate one with
.B "tacctl user passwd " <name>
(setting a real password implicitly enables the account).
.B root
is different — a permanent accounting-only sink whose hash stays
disabled for the lifetime of the install. It exists because Junos
internal daemons (mgd, jsd, op-script probes) run non-tty CLI commands
as root and generate accounting packets; without a
.B root
entry tacquito logs an error for every packet.
.B "tacctl user passwd root"
is rejected — root is a Junos built-in intended for local/console use
only and must never authenticate via TACACS+.
.TP
.BI "upgrade [--branch " name "]"
Move tacctl state into
.I /etc/tacctl
(idempotent), pull and rebuild tacquito when upstream or the patch overlay
changed, pull the management scripts (and re-execute when tacctl itself
changed), install packages the new release needs, bring every enabled
backend's configuration in line with the release (a re-render from the
store; RADIUS restarts when its files or unit drop-in changed), refresh
system files, move an install that has no store yet into the store behind a
gate, and restart tacquito once if anything changed (rolling the binary and
unit files back if it does not come up). Listener, log-level and metrics
settings live in
.I tacctl.yaml
and survive. See
.BR "UPGRADING TO 0.1.15" .
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove tacctl and every backend that is enabled or installed: the tacquito
service, units, binaries, configuration and logs; tacctl's FreeRADIUS
configuration, unit drop-in and logs (the FreeRADIUS package stays, with its
own configuration as shipped); the state directory, sudoers rules,
completion, man page and management repository. Offers to preserve backups
and each backend's logs by archiving them to
.IR /root/ .
.TP
.B status
Show service health, authentication statistics, recent errors, and
password-age warnings. With more than one backend enabled each backend has a
labelled section
.RB ( "== Backend: <id> ..." )
holding its service, config, accounting log, drift and activity lines; the
users, backups, security posture and password-age lines that belong to no
backend stay in the general part. With one backend enabled the report is
laid out as it always was. A listener on an IPv6 network
.RB ( tcp6 ", " udp6 )
of any backend triggers the IPv6 ACL parity check.
.TP
.BR "version" ", " "--version" ", " "-v"
Print the tacctl version. The version is derived from
.BR git-describe (1)
against the installed repository, so it reflects the exact commit
currently deployed.
.SS User management \fB(tacctl user)\fR
.TP
.B user list
List all users with name, group, status, and password age.
.TP
.BI "user show " name
Show detailed user info: group, status, password change date and age,
last login (the most recent of every enabled backend: the TACACS+
accounting log, the RADIUS auth log), Cisco privilege level, Juniper
class, and a hash fingerprint. Read-only -- does not prompt for the
password (unlike
.BR "user verify" ).
.TP
.BI "user add " "name group " [ "--hash " hash ] [ "--scopes " name[,name...] ]
Add a user. Prompts for the password with confirmation. If
.BI --hash " hash"
is given, the bcrypt hash is used directly and no password is prompted
(use this when users self-generate a hash; see
.BR "tacctl hash commands" ).
.BI --scopes " name"[,name...]
grants the user access to those scopes (see
.B SCOPE MANAGEMENT ).
Without
.BR --scopes ,
the user lands in the current default scope. On fresh installs the
default scope is
.B lab
— least-privilege posture, so an operator who forgets
.B --scopes
does not accidentally grant production access.
.PP
Reserved usernames
.RB ( root
and
.BR tacquito )
are rejected: both map to local OS accounts that tacquito resolves outside
the YAML. Create an operator-specific account instead (for example,
.BR "tacctl user add jsmith superuser" ).
.TP
.BI "user remove " name
Remove a user after confirmation.
.TP
.B passwd
Change your own password. Takes no arguments: the target is the tacctl
user whose name matches the login that invoked
.BR sudo (8).
The current password must verify before the new one is accepted, and the
new one is held to the same strength policy as
.BR "user passwd" .
Available to every tier.
.TP
.BI "user passwd " "name " [ "--hash " hash ]
Change a user's password. Prompts for the new password with
confirmation. If
.BI --hash " hash"
is given, the bcrypt hash is used directly and no password is prompted
(mirrors
.B user add --hash
for users who self-generate via
.BR "tacctl hash commands" ).
.TP
.BI "user disable " name
Disable a user while preserving the password hash for later re-enable.
.TP
.BI "user enable " name
Re-enable a previously disabled user.
.TP
.BI "user rename " "old new"
Rename a user.
.TP
.BI "user move " "name group"
Move a user to a different group without changing the password.
.TP
.BI "user verify " name
Show user details and verify the password interactively.
.TP
.B "user scope " \fIname\fR " " [ "list " | " add " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " remove " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " set " \fIs\fR[,s...] | " clear " ]
Manage which scopes
.I name
can authenticate from. With no subcommand (or
.BR list ),
prints the user's current scopes with orphan detection (any scope
name that does not resolve to an existing scope is flagged red).
.B add
and
.B remove
accept one or a comma-separated list of existing scope names;
.B set
replaces the whole list;
.B clear
empties it (with confirmation).
.B clear
preserves the bcrypt hash — distinct from
.B user disable
in that intent is "temporarily revoke scope access" rather than
"revoke the password".
.SS Group management \fB(tacctl group)\fR
.TP
.B group list
List all groups with Cisco privilege level, Juniper class, and user count.
.TP
.BI "group add " "name priv-lvl juniper-class"
Add a custom group.
.I priv-lvl
is an integer 0\(en15;
.I juniper-class
matches the Junos login class name.
.TP
.B "group edit " \fIname\fR " { priv-lvl \fIN\fR | juniper-class \fICLASS\fR }"
Change a group's Cisco privilege level or Juniper class. The built-in
groups
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser )
can be edited but not removed.
.TP
.BI "group remove " name
Remove a custom group. Built-in groups are protected.
.TP
.B "group commands list " \fIgroup\fR
Show the per-command authorization rules for a group, including the
default action (the action of the trailing
.RB ` * `
catchall rule).
.TP
.B "group commands default " \fIgroup\fR " { permit | deny }"
Set the default action for unmatched commands. Stored as the action
of a trailing
.RB ` * `
rule that tacquito's
.B CommandBasedAuthorizer
matches as a wildcard.
.TP
.B "group commands add " \fIgroup\fR " " \fIname\fR " [ --match \fIregex\fR ]... [ --action permit | deny ]"
Append a rule. Default action is
.BR permit .
The
.I name
is matched against the TACACS+
.B cmd=
field literally. Each
.B --match
adds a regex that tacquito tests against the command's
.I arguments
only: the space-joined
.B cmd-arg
values after the command word (auto-anchored with
.RB ` ^…$ `).
For
.B show running-config
the string tested is
.BR running-config ,
never the full line, so a regex that repeats the command word
.RB ( "^show .*$" )
can never match and is rejected. Omit
.B --match
to cover any arguments.
The first matching rule wins; tacquito returns FAIL if no rule
matches and the
.RB ` * `
catchall is not
.BR permit .
.TP
.B "group commands remove " \fIgroup\fR " " \fIname\fR
Drop a rule by name. The
.RB ` * `
catchall is protected; change it via
.B group commands default
or remove the entire block via
.B group commands clear .
.TP
.BI "group commands clear " group
Remove a group's command-rule override from
.IR tacctl.yaml .
The merged view falls back to the shipped defaults for built-in groups
.RB ( readonly ,
.BR operator ,
.BR superuser );
for custom groups the group ends up with no rules at all.
.I tacquito.yaml
is regenerated afterwards so device-side enforcement stays in sync.
Confirmation required.
.TP
.B "group commands seed " [ \fIgroup\fR ] " " [ --force ]
Recovery tool. Writes the legacy broader rule set (with
.BR enable ,
.BR clear ,
.BR test ,
.BR monitor ,
etc.) as overrides in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
On fresh installs the smaller shipped defaults (viewable via
.BR "tacctl config defaults" )
are already in effect; you only need
.B seed
if you've customized and want to start from a known, broader baseline.
Without an argument, seeds all three built-ins; with a group name, only
that one (custom groups are not seeded). Refuses to overwrite existing
rules without
.BR --force .
Legacy seed defaults:
.B readonly
permits
.IR "show ping traceroute terminal exit end quit logout who where enable"
with default deny;
.B operator
adds
.IR "clear test monitor"
to the readonly set with default deny;
.B superuser
gets a
.RB ` "* permit" `
catchall.
.TP
.BI "group privilege list " group
Show the Cisco
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings for a group. Reports whether the source is an explicit
override (in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
under
.BR privileges.\fIgroup\fR )
or the built-in default set (move-DOWN commands only).
.TP
.B "group privilege add " \fIgroup\fR " '\fIcmd\fR'[,'\fIcmd\fR'...]"
Move one or more Cisco commands to the group's priv-lvl. Quote
multi-word commands (e.g.
.RB ' "show running-config" ' );
separate multiple commands with commas. On first
.B add
the group's defaults are inherited; subsequent adds extend that set.
Every command is validated before anything is written — a bad command
aborts the whole op.
.TP
.B "group privilege remove " \fIgroup\fR " '\fIcmd\fR'[,'\fIcmd\fR'...]"
Drop one or more previously-mapped commands. Accepts the same
comma-separated list form as
.BR add .
If only defaults are in effect, the remove takes effect against the
default set (suppressing those commands for that group).
.TP
.BI "group privilege clear " group
Wipe explicit mappings for a group; the built-in default set takes
over again.
.TP
.B "group privilege seed " [ \fIgroup\fR ] " " [ --force ]
Populate the built-in groups with their default mapping set. Same
shape as
.BR "group commands seed" .
Default sets:
.B readonly
none (priv-1 is the floor);
.B operator
ships a priv-15 read/diagnostic family moved DOWN to priv-7
.RI ( "show running-config" ,
.IR "show startup-config" ,
.IR "show tech-support" ,
.IR "show archive" ,
.IR "show access-list" ,
.IR "show ip route" );
.B superuser
none (priv-15 is the ceiling).
.PP
Cisco devices enforce these rules LIVE over TACACS+ — once any group has rules,
.B tacctl config cisco
emits
.B aaa authorization commands 1/7/15 default group TACACS-GROUP local
and IOS asks tacquito for every command. Juniper enforces locally via
class
.B allow-commands
/
.B deny-commands
regex; a per-device config push is required after every change. RADIUS
has no per-command authorization: the server does not enforce these rules
for logins over RADIUS
.RB ( status ,
.B "config render"
and
.B "backend enable"
name the groups concerned).
.SS Server configuration \fB(tacctl config)\fR
.TP
.B config show
Show the current configuration summary. Each enabled backend adds its config
file, service state and the listeners it should have, probed on the port
(and protocol) of the listener, not on port 49; with more than one backend
enabled each has a labelled block.
.TP
.B config dump
Print the tacctl tunable posture: canonical defaults
.RB ( conf_emit_defaults
in
.IR lib/conf.sh ),
operator overrides
.RI ( tacctl.yaml ),
and the effective merged view.
.TP
.B config defaults
Print the canonical defaults YAML only (no overrides, no merge).
Useful for seeding a new
.I tacctl.yaml
or piping into
.B diff
against an existing one.
.TP
.BI "config get " "path" " [fallback]"
Read one dotted-path value (e.g.
.BR bcrypt.cost )
from the merged view. Emits empty + the fallback if the path is missing.
Lists and maps produce no output; use
.B "config get-list"
or
.B "config dump"
instead.
.TP
.BI "config get-list " "path"
Read a list value (e.g.
.BR mgmt_acl.permits )
from the merged view, one element per stdout line.
.TP
.B config validate
Validate the store
.RI ( store.yaml :
names, every user's group and scopes exist, each prefix owned by one scope),
YAML syntax for
.I tacquito.yaml
and
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
schema-walk every override key in
.I tacctl.yaml
against the write-time validator (catches hand-edits that bypass the
setter commands — including malformed
.BR commands.\fIgroup\fR
rules,
.BR privileges.\fIgroup\fR
lists, out-of-range integers, unknown keys), then check the server config
for structural issues: missing sections, orphan scope refs, default-scope
marker pointing at a nonexistent scope, per-scope key divergence,
reserved usernames
.RB ( root ,
.BR tacquito )
present as tacctl-managed users, users missing the
.B accounter:
field, and per-scope key divergence.
.IP
Then, for every enabled backend, whether its rendered config is up to date
with the store and whether any of its files was edited by hand (drift). With
more than one backend enabled each gets a labelled block, and a drifted file of
one backend does not hide another's state; files left behind by a disabled
backend are not checked. With RADIUS enabled it warns about a scope RADIUS
serves that sends no vendor attribute and is not a Linux-host scope. Exits 1
when it finds errors.
.TP
.B "config render " [ \-\-force ]
Render every enabled backend's config (for TACACS+:
.I tacquito.yaml
and the systemd drop-ins) from the store and
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
all backends or none, and restart the ones whose files changed. Needed after
hand-restoring files or when
.B config validate
reports a config that is missing or out of date. Refuses to overwrite an
artifact that was edited by hand (drift); with
.B \-\-force
it replaces it and keeps a copy under
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ .
.TP
.BI "config diff " [ timestamp ]
Alias of
.BR "backup diff" .
.TP
.BI "config restore " "timestamp " [ \-\-legacy ]
Alias of
.BR "backup restore" .
.TP
.BI "config loglevel " [ debug | info | error ]
Show or change the TACACS+ server's log level. Stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( backends.tacacs.level ),
so the change survives upgrades.
.TP
.B "config listen " "[ \-\-backend \fIid\fR ] [ \-\-listener \fIname\fR ] { show | tcp | tcp6 | udp | udp6 | reset } " [ address ]
Show, change, or reset a listen address (default: the
.B tacacs
backend's listener
.BR default ;
the backend ids are listed by
.BR "backend list" ).
Stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( listeners. \fIbackend\fB. \fIname\fR),
from which tacctl renders the TACACS+ units' systemd drop-ins and the
RADIUS
.B listen
sections.
.B reset
clears the override and falls back to the built-in default.
With
.BI \-\-listener " name"
the command addresses another TACACS+ listener, which runs as its own unit
.BI tacquito@ name .service
with its own accounting log; there
.B reset
removes it. TACACS+ listeners take
.B tcp
or
.BR tcp6 ;
RADIUS ones
.RB ( "\-\-backend radius" )
take
.B udp
or
.B udp6
and are named: the built-in
.B auth
(udp :1812) and
.B acct
(udp :1813); a further name is an authentication listener. The unit is
restarted and must stay up, or
.I tacctl.yaml
and the drop-ins are put back. A listener's
.B tls
block in
.I tacctl.yaml
is reserved:
.B "tls.enabled: true"
is rejected (TACACS+ over TLS is not implemented).
.B
Switching to tcp6
prompts for confirmation because dual-stack sockets accept IPv4
clients via mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:x.x.x.x )
that silently bypass IPv4 prefix ACLs; see
.B NOTES.
.TP
.B "config metrics " "{ show | enable | disable | address " \fIhost:port\fR " | reset }"
Control tacquito's prometheus HTTP exporter's listen address (TACACS+ only;
FreeRADIUS has no exporter). Stored as
.BR backends.tacacs.metrics_address .
.B show
(no arg) prints current state and address.
.B enable
clears the override and reverts to the unit default
.RB ( 127.0.0.1:8080 ,
loopback-only).
.B disable
sinks the exporter to
.B 127.0.0.1:0
— an ephemeral loopback port that no scraper can discover, so the
exporter is effectively off even though its goroutine still runs. This
indirection is deliberate: tacquito's upstream
.B -export-promhttp=false
path unconditionally cancels the server context, which would tear the
whole daemon down, so tacctl avoids the flag and uses an unreachable
bind instead.
.B "address " \fIhost:port\fR
binds explicitly (e.g.
.B 10.1.0.1:8080
to expose to external scrapers); non-loopback binds print a warning.
.B reset
clears the override and falls back to the unit default.
.TP
.B "host list"
Show enrolled Linux hosts with their SSH target, scope, server address,
method and user count.
.TP
.B "host enroll " "{ [" user "@]" host " | \-\-local } [ \-\-method tacplus|radius ] [ \-\-scope " name " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-name " name " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-identity " file " ] [ \-\-build\-on\-host ] [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-adopt " names " ]"
Copy the
.B "config linux"
install script to a host over SSH, run it there as root, and register the
host. The method is
.B tacplus
(pam_tacplus against the TACACS+ backend) or
.B radius
(the distribution's pam_radius_auth package against the RADIUS backend:
.B libpam\-radius\-auth
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.B pam_radius
from EPEL on the RHEL family, where the script enables EPEL when the host
does not have it, and stops before any account or PAM change when it cannot
install the package). It is, in order:
.BR \-\-method ;
the method the host is registered with; the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" ;
the one protocol the scope's
.B protocols
filter names;
.B "host default-method"
(default
.BR tacplus ).
The method's backend must be enabled and the scope must allow its
protocol. Re-enrolling a registered host with the other method switches it:
the first method's module, secret file and SELinux module are removed
(packages stay). Without
.B \-\-scope
a scope named
.BI linux\- name
is created for the host's own address (/32) with a generated secret and
.B protocols
set to the method's protocol, so a
secret read off one host is useless from another. The server address
defaults to the source address of the route to the host.
.B \-\-local
enrolls this machine without SSH. ssh runs as the user who invoked
.BR sudo (8),
using their keys; the remote login must be root or able to sudo. The
remote copy of the script is deleted after the run. Nothing is registered
if the remote run fails.
.IP
For
.BR tacplus ,
pam_tacplus is built on this server, in a rootless
.BR podman (1)
container matching the host's OS release, cached, and shipped as a binary;
Debian, Ubuntu and RHEL-family hosts (RHEL, AlmaLinux, Rocky, CentOS
Stream, Oracle Linux) are supported, the latter built against AlmaLinux of
the same major release;
the host compiles it from source instead when no image matches its OS, its
architecture differs, the container build fails, or
.B \-\-build\-on\-host
is given. A host that already has the module from the same source is not
touched. Requires
.B "config linux build"
to have been run. For
.B radius
none of this applies
.RB ( \-\-build\-on\-host
warns that it is not applicable). Both methods are tested on Ubuntu 24.04,
Debian 12 and 13, AlmaLinux and Rocky Linux 8, 9 and 10; SELinux in
enforcing mode is not verified.
.IP
A reject from the server is final. When the server does not answer (or the
host's shared secret is wrong), login falls through to the local password,
which only adopted accounts have; local administrators outside
.B tac\-users
never reach the server. With the server silent a user without a local
password is refused after about 5\(en6 s (tacplus) or 8\(en9 s (radius).
pam_radius_auth 2.0.x on Ubuntu 24.04 and Debian 12 neither sends nor checks
Message-Authenticator (BlastRADIUS, CVE-2024-3596); elsewhere tacctl adds
.BR require_message_authenticator .
Session accounting over RADIUS is sent only when the RADIUS
.B acct
listener is on the authentication port plus one, and never from Ubuntu
24.04 (malformed records).
.TP
.B "host sync " "{ " name " | \-\-all } [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-adopt " names " ]"
Push the scope's current user list to the host: new users get accounts,
tier changes move group membership, and users no longer in the scope lose
the tac-* groups (accounts tacctl created are expired, which also blocks
SSH-key logins). Run after
.BR "user add" ", " "user remove" ", " "user move" " or " "user scope"
changes.
.IP
If the host already has a local account under a TACACS+ user's name that
tacctl did not create, enroll and sync stop before changing anything
unless the name is given with
.BI \-\-adopt " name" [, name ...].
An adopted account keeps its UID, local password, files and groups. A
warning is printed if it belongs to a privileged local group, and again
when the user is later removed or disabled: adopted accounts are not
locked or expired, so the account stays usable locally until blocked by
hand.
.TP
.B "host unenroll " name " [ \-\-force ]"
Run the removal script on the host and drop it from the registry. Local
accounts and home directories are left in place. If the removal fails the
host stays registered unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.BR "host default-method " [ tacplus | radius ]
Show or set the method for hosts enrolled without
.B \-\-method
whose scope decides none
.RB ( host.default_method
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
default
.BR tacplus ).
Warns when the method's backend is not enabled.
.TP
.B "config linux build"
Fetch the pinned
.B pam_tacplus
release (v1.7.0, verified by commit hash) and prepare a self-contained
source tarball under
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/linux .
Needs autoconf, automake, libtool, gnulib and the PAM headers on the
server. Run once before generating
.B tacplus
scripts (the
.B radius
method needs nothing built).
.TP
.B "config linux script " "[ \-\-scope " name " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-method tacplus|radius ] [ \-\-output " file " ]"
Write an install script that sets up TACACS+ or RADIUS login on a Debian,
Ubuntu or RHEL-family host in the given scope (method as for
.BR "host enroll" ,
without the registered host). The script embeds the scope's shared secret (it
is written mode 0600; delete it after use), for
.B tacplus
the source tarball, and the
scope's users with their tier and a stable UID. Run as root on the target
it installs the module (tacplus: builds it; radius: installs the
distribution's package), creates a local account per user (locked password)
in the groups
.BR tac-users " and " tac-readonly ", " tac-operator " or " tac-superuser ,
points the
.BR sshd ", " sudo ", " sudo\-i ", " login ", " sddm " and " gdm\-password
PAM services (where present) at the server for members of
.B tac-users
only, and grants
.B tac-superuser
full sudo. Accounts that already exist are adopted with their local
password left intact. Other local accounts are not affected. It refuses
to run when no local administrator with a usable password exists outside
the TACACS+ user list, and puts the PAM files back if any step fails.
Re-run it with
.B \-\-accounts\-only
to sync users after changes on the server.
.TP
.B "config linux uid " "[ " username " [ " uid " ] ]"
List the UID assigned to each user, print one user's UID, or change it.
Each user has one number, used as both UID and primary GID on every
enrolled host; it is assigned the first time the user is sent to a host
and kept in
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids .
The install and sync scripts stop before changing anything if that number
is already used on a host by another user or group, and print the ways to
resolve it: renumber the conflicting account on the host, assign the
tacctl user a different number with this command, or accept a different
number on that host with
.BR \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch .
Changing a UID does not renumber accounts that already exist on hosts.
Accounts that existed before enrollment are adopted with their own UID,
and a difference from the assigned one is reported on every sync.
.TP
.B "config linux builds " "[ list | clear ]"
List or delete the pam_tacplus modules that
.B "host enroll"
built in containers, one per OS release and architecture, kept in
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/linux/builds .
The listing shows the digest of the base image each was built from.
.TP
.B "config linux remove-script " "[ \-\-output " file " ]"
Write a script that removes TACACS+ or RADIUS login from a host (either
method): it undoes the PAM edits, deletes the files holding the shared
secret, the module, the SELinux module and the sudoers drop-in, and reports
accounts left with no way to log in. Local accounts, home directories, the
tac-* groups and installed packages are left in place.
.TP
.B "config sudoers " "{ show | install | remove } " [ group ]
Manage the optional
.I /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl
drop-in that grants NOPASSWD sudo on
.B tacctl
to a group (default:
.BR adm ).
Installs are validated with
.BR visudo (8)
before taking effect. Not installed by default.
.TP
.B "config sudoers tiers " "{ show | install | remove }"
Manage
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl-tiers ,
which grants tacctl by tier to tacctl users that have local accounts.
Members of
.B tac-readonly
may run
.BR passwd ", " status ", " version ", " "user list" ", " "user show" ", "
.BR "group list" ", " "scope list" ", " "backend list" " and " "backend status" .
Members of
.B tac-operator
may additionally run
.BR "log tail" ", " "log search" ", " "log failures" ", " "log accounting" ", "
.BR "config validate" " and " "backup list" .
Members of
.B tac-superuser
get full
.BR sudo (8)
access. Lower-tier rules are NOPASSWD. Commands that print a shared
secret or a password hash are superuser-only. tacctl re-checks the
caller's tier itself on every run, reading the tier from the user's group
in the store
(priv-lvl 15 superuser, 7\-14 operator, below 7 read-only); callers
outside the local group
.B tac-users
are not tier-restricted.
.TP
.BI "config password-age " [ days ]
Show or set the password age warning threshold. Default 90 days.
.TP
.BI "config bcrypt-cost " [ "10-14" ]
Show or set the bcrypt cost factor used when hashing new or changed
passwords. Default 12. Existing hashes keep their minted cost.
.TP
.BI "config password-min-length " [ "8-64" ]
Show or set the minimum length enforced by
.B validate_password_strength
on interactively-entered passwords. Default 12 (OWASP 2025). Floor 8
(NIST 800-63). Auto-generated passwords (operator accepts the blank
prompt) bypass this check.
.TP
.BI "config secret-min-length " [ "16-128" ]
Show or set the minimum length enforced when an operator types a
custom shared secret via
.BR "scope secret " \fIname\fR " set" .
Default 16 (Cisco TACACS+ best practice). Auto-generated secrets
bypass this check.
.TP
.B "config allow " "{ list | add | remove | clear } " [ cidr[,cidr...] ]
Manage the connection allow list (IP ACL).
.B add
and
.B remove
accept a single CIDR or a comma-separated list.
.B clear
wipes the entire list (with confirmation) — the result fails open so
any source IP becomes eligible to connect, subject to the deny list
and the secret-provider prefixes.
.TP
.B "config deny " "{ list | add | remove | clear } " [ cidr[,cidr...] ]
Manage the connection deny list. Same comma-list and
.B clear
semantics as
.BR "config allow" .
.B deny
takes precedence over
.BR allow ;
with both empty, all connections are accepted.
.TP
.B "config mgmt-acl " "{ list | add | remove | clear | cisco-name | juniper-name } " [ arg ]
Manage a shared CIDR permit list stored under
.B mgmt_acl.permits
in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
that drives both the Cisco VTY-ACL and a commented Juniper lo0
firewall-filter example emitted by
.B config cisco
and
.BR "config juniper" .
Preserved across
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.B list
/
.B add
/
.B remove
/
.B clear
manage the permit list;
.B add
and
.B remove
accept a single CIDR or a comma-separated list.
.B cisco-name
and
.B juniper-name
configure the emitted ACL/filter names (see below). With no arguments,
prints usage, the current entry count, and both ACL names.
.TP
.BI "config mgmt-acl cisco-name " [ name ]
Show or set the name of the IOS access-list emitted by
.BR "config cisco" .
Default
.BR VTY-ACL .
Names must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits,
.RB ` _ ` ,
or
.RB ` - `
(max 63 chars).
.TP
.BI "config mgmt-acl juniper-name " [ name ]
Show or set the name of the Junos firewall filter emitted by
.BR "config juniper" .
Default
.BR MGMT-ACL .
Same character rules as
.BR cisco-name .
Both overrides live under
.B mgmt_acl.names.{cisco,juniper}
in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
and survive
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.TP
.BI "config cisco " [ "--scope " name "] [--legacy] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a ready-to-paste Cisco IOS/IOS-XE configuration with the
server IP, shared secret, current group-to-privilege mappings, and
the VTY-ACL built from
.B config mgmt-acl
pre-filled. Without
.BR --scope ,
emits the default scope's secret (header names the scope and lists
sibling scopes as a hint). With
.BR --legacy ,
emits legacy IOS 12.x syntax
.RB ( "tacacs-server host" " and " "aaa group server \&... / server <ip>" )
for devices predating the structured
.B "tacacs server"
block introduced in IOS 15.0.
.IP
With
.B "--protocol radius"
(default: the scope's auth-method, see
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
else the one protocol its
.B protocols
filter names, else
.BR tacacs )
the configuration is for the RADIUS backend instead (see
.B RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
below);
.B --legacy
is TACACS+ only.
.TP
.BI "config juniper " [ "--scope " name "] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a ready-to-paste Juniper Junos configuration with template
users, TACACS+ server setup, verification commands, and a commented
lo0 firewall-filter example built from
.B config mgmt-acl
pre-filled. Scope handling matches
.BR "config cisco" .
.B "--protocol radius"
renders the RADIUS configuration (see
.B RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
below).
.TP
.BI "config wti " [ "--scope " name "] [--protocol " tacacs | radius ]
Print a step-by-step procedure for the serial text interface of a WTI
console server (DSM/CPM/REM/TSM/RSM, firmware v8.x): the
.B /N
Network Parameters menu, the TACACS Parameters item, and the value to
key in for every field (server IP, Secret Word, port 49, Fallback Local
derived from the scope's
.BR aaa-order ,
Default User Access
.B On
with Access Level ViewOnly (required: with it Off the unit's OpenSSH
treats TACACS-only users as invalid and sends a junk password),
Account/Session Management Modules, Service Name
.BR shell ),
an IP Tables check (the unit must accept ESTABLISHED,RELATED traffic or
tacquito's replies are dropped),
followed by the group-to-access-level mapping, a troubleshooting step
that turns on the unit's TACACS Debug echo, and the matching tacquito
log lines to look for. WTI reads the standard
.B priv-lvl
attribute from the same
.B shell
service Cisco uses (0\-4 ViewOnly, 5\-9 User, 10\-14 SuperUser, 15
Administrator), so no extra service block is needed. The unit's
accounting (Session Management Module) needs tacquito built with the
.I patches/0002
overlay, which sends an empty server_msg on accounting success; the
unit drops the SSH session when it receives upstream's message. Warns when the
scope secret or a scope member's username is unlikely to survive the
unit's menu entry (whitespace, punctuation, over 32 characters). Scope
handling and the protocol (flag, auth-method, the scope's only protocol,
TACACS+) match
.BR "config cisco" .
.IP
Over RADIUS the walkthrough is for the unit's RADIUS Parameters menu
.RB ( /N ,
item 29 in WTI's user guide) and prints the group to
.B WTI-Super
table (0 ViewOnly, 1 User, 2 SuperUser, 3 Administrator, in the bands
above). It has
.B not
been verified on a unit: it is written from WTI's documents
.RI ( docs/radius-notes.md ),
which disagree on the level a login gets when the reply carries no
.BR WTI-Super .
Refused, as for Cisco and Juniper, when the scope sends no
.B WTI-Super
(see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
.TP
.BI "config branch " [ name ]
Show or change the tacctl repository branch used by
.B tacctl upgrade.
.SS Auth backends \fB(tacctl backend)\fR
A backend is a daemon that serves the store over one protocol:
.B tacacs
(TACACS+ served by tacquito, installed by
.BR install )
and
.B radius
(RADIUS served by FreeRADIUS, opt-in); which are enabled is
.B backends.enabled
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(default:
.BR tacacs ).
See
.BR BACKENDS .
.TP
.B backend list
Every backend this tacctl has: protocol, implementation, whether it is
installed, whether it is enabled, and its service state. Read-only.
.TP
.BI "backend status " [ id ]
Service and listener state of every backend (or one): state, uptime, PID,
and for each listener its network and address, the state of its unit and
whether something is listening there. Read-only.
.TP
.BI "backend enable " "id " [ \-y ]
Enable a backend. If it is not installed, confirms (\fB\-y\fR skips) and runs its
install steps (packages, service account, unit). Then, as one step: every
backend that will be enabled has its gate checked (a hand-edited rendered file
refuses the command, exit 3), a snapshot is taken, the id is added to
.BR backends.enabled ,
and the config of every enabled backend is rendered, all or none. Then the
service is enabled at boot and started, and must report
.BR active .
If any step fails, the files tacctl owns
.RB ( tacctl.yaml ,
the store, every rendered file) are left or put back exactly as they were, the
service is stopped and disabled, and the exit status is 1; software that the
install step put on the machine stays.
.TP
.BI "backend disable " "id " [ \-y ]
Take a backend out of
.BR backends.enabled ,
stop its service and disable it at boot. Asks for confirmation (\fB\-y\fR
skips) because clients of that protocol stop authenticating. Its package and
the files it rendered stay where they are (and are no longer rendered or
reported as drift);
.B "backend enable"
brings it back. Refused for the last enabled backend (nothing would serve the
store: enable another first, or use
.BR uninstall ),
and for
.B tacacs
on an install that has no store yet (legacy read-only mode, where
.I tacquito.yaml
is still the source of truth).
.PP
.B backend list
and
.B backend status
are available to the read-only and operator tiers;
.B enable
and
.B disable
are superuser only.
.SS Canonical store \fB(tacctl store)\fR
.TP
.BR "store show " [ \-\-json ]
Print the model (users, groups, scopes, filters) as YAML or JSON.
.B Superuser only:
the output contains shared secrets and password hashes.
.TP
.B "store import " "[ \-\-check | \-\-force ] [ \-\-replace ] [ " file " ]"
Import an old-style
.I tacquito.yaml
(default: the live one) into the store.
.B \-\-check
writes nothing and reports what would happen and whether the rendered config
is equivalent;
.B \-\-force
drops content the store cannot represent (each item is listed);
.B \-\-replace
overwrites an existing store. Also the way to keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
that
.B config validate
reports as drift.
.TP
.B store rollback
Undo the move into the store: restore the pre-store
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
remove the store, restart. The install is back in legacy read-only mode.
.SS Log inspection \fB(tacctl log)\fR
With more than one backend enabled each log subcommand shows every backend's
log in a section headed by its name; with one, the output is unchanged.
.B \-\-backend
.I id
(any subcommand, anywhere on the line) limits it to one backend. TACACS+
logs to the journal of its units and to
.IR /var/log/tacquito/accounting*.log ;
RADIUS to
.I tacctl\-auth.log
(one line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject, with scope, client, NAS and
reason),
.I tacctl\-accounting.log
and
.I tacctl\-radius.log
in the package's log directory.
.TP
.BI "log tail " [ n ]
Show the last
.I n
entries (default 20): the tacquito journal; the RADIUS auth log and daemon log.
.TP
.BI "log search " term
Search logs for a username or keyword (TACACS+: over the last 7 days).
.TP
.B log failures
Show authentication failures from the last 24 hours.
.TP
.BI "log accounting " [ n ]
Show the last
.I n
accounting records
.RI ( /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log ;
the RADIUS detail file).
.TP
.B "log clear " [ --force ]
Purge tacquito journal entries
.RB ( "journalctl --rotate && journalctl --vacuum-time=1s -u tacquito" )
and truncate the TACACS+ accounting logs; truncate the RADIUS auth,
accounting and daemon logs.
Prompts for confirmation;
.B --force
(or
.BR -y )
skips the prompt for scripted use. Destructive — historical auth and
accounting records are irrecoverable once cleared.
.SS Backup management \fB(tacctl backup)\fR
.TP
.B backup list
List snapshots, newest first, then old-style backups.
.TP
.BI "backup diff " [ timestamp ]
Diff
.I store.yaml
and
.I tacctl.yaml
against a snapshot (default: most recent). Superuser only: the output
shows shared secrets and password hashes.
.TP
.BI "backup restore " timestamp " " [ \-\-legacy ]
Restore both files from a snapshot after confirmation, re-render every
enabled backend's config (all or none), and restart the backends. The current
state is snapshotted first, so a restore can itself be undone. Nothing changes
if the snapshot is invalid or the render fails. A snapshot's
.B backends.enabled
comes back with the rest of
.IR tacctl.yaml :
a backend it enables must already be installed (otherwise nothing changes and
the message says to run
.BR "backend enable" ),
is enabled at boot and started; one it leaves out is stopped and disabled. With
.B \-\-legacy
the argument names an old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
file, which goes through
.B store import \-\-check
before it is imported.
.PP
A snapshot is the directory
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/<timestamp>/
holding
.IR store.yaml ,
.I tacctl.yaml
and a manifest (the
.I rendered.json
of that moment and the tacctl version), root-only. One is taken
automatically before every change, unless the files already equal the
newest snapshot. The newest 30 are retained. Without a store, list, diff
and restore work on old-style backups only.
.SS Hash generation \fB(tacctl hash)\fR
.TP
.B hash
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary listing
.B generate
and
.BR commands .
Does not require root.
.TP
.B hash generate
Interactively prompt for a password on the server and print its bcrypt
hash. Needs
.B python3-bcrypt
installed. Intended for admins who can shell in to the tacquito host.
.TP
.B hash commands
Print client-side bcrypt-generation recipes for operators who cannot
SSH to the server. Covers Linux/macOS (Python + htpasswd), Windows
(Python + PowerShell/BCrypt.Net), and any-OS Node.js. Each recipe
prints both the raw
.B $2b$\&...
and hex-encoded forms;
.B "user add --hash"
/
.B "user passwd --hash"
accept either and normalize to hex on write.
.SH SCOPE MANAGEMENT
A
.I scope
is a named bundle of (client CIDR prefixes, shared secret), kept in the
store and shared by every backend: the same prefixes and secret serve
TACACS+ and RADIUS, unless the scope's
.B protocols
filter limits it. Each user's
.B scopes
list references one or more of these by name. A client is matched to the
scope with the most specific prefix that contains its address (tacquito
walks its rendered
.B secrets:
list, sorted by specificity, first match wins; FreeRADIUS takes the longest
prefix); authentication succeeds only when that scope is in the user's
list.
.PP
Fresh installs seed a single scope named
.B lab
(least-privilege default). Operators add additional scopes (for
example
.BR prod ,
.BR edge ,
.BR dmz )
as needed, and place users into them explicitly.
.SS Top-level \fB(tacctl scope)\fR
.TP
.B scope list
One row per scope (deduplicated), with each scope's prefixes joined
as a comma list. Handy for a high-level inventory — names, prefix
counts, user counts, and default marker. For the first-match routing
view (one row per scope-prefix pair, numbered by slice position,
mirroring how tacquito routes a connecting client) use
.BR "scope routing" .
For full per-scope detail (secret, per-scope knobs, user list) use
.BR "scope show " \fIname\fR .
.TP
.B scope routing
Print every (scope, prefix) pair in first-match order, as rendered into
tacquito's
.B secrets[]
list (one row per scope-prefix pair,
numbered by slice position). Multi-prefix scopes repeat their name on
each prefix row so the display literally mirrors routing priority —
reading top to bottom shows the order tacquito tries providers for a
connecting client.
.TP
.BI "scope show " name
Full detail for one scope: prefixes, users that reference it, raw
shared-secret value with length/posture, whether the scope is
the default, its protocols filter and auth-method, its vendor attributes
and tagged addresses (RADIUS).
.TP
.B "scope add " \fIname\fR " --prefixes " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " [ --secret \fIvalue\fR | --secret generate ] [ --protocols \fIcsv\fR ] [ --vendor-attrs \fIcsv\fR ] [ --default ]
Create a new scope. Prefixes are required; each must not already
belong to another scope (overlap with a broader scope's supernet is
fine — the new scope's narrower prefix will win first-match).
.B --secret generate
produces a strong key automatically.
.B --default
makes the new scope the default for
.BR "user add" .
.B --protocols
and
.B --vendor-attrs
set what
.B "scope protocols set"
and
.B "scope vendor-attrs enable"
would.
.TP
.BI "scope remove " "name " [ --force ]
Remove a scope from the store (and so from every backend's rendered
config). Refuses by default if any user references the
scope; with
.BR --force ,
strips the name from all affected users AND removes the scope,
with a loud warning. Its vendor attributes and tagged addresses go with it. Every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the scope's name
.RB ( aaa.order ,
.BR exec_timeout ,
.BR tacacs_group ,
.BR radius_group ,
.BR scope_auth_method ,
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco|juniper
and
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits )
is removed with it, so a new scope of that name starts from the defaults.
.TP
.BI "scope rename " "old new"
Rename a scope. Rewrites the scope in the store (protocols, vendor
attributes and tagged addresses included), every user's
.B scopes
reference, and
.B scope.default
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(if it pointed at
.IR old ),
and moves every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the old name to the new one.
.TP
.BI "scope default " [ name ]
Show (no arg) or set (with arg) the default scope. Stored under
.B scope.default
in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Used by
.BR "user add"
when
.B --scopes
is omitted, and by
.BR "config cisco" " / " "config juniper" " / " "config wti"
when
.B --scope
is omitted.
.TP
.BI "scope lookup " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Resolve an IPv4 / IPv6 address or CIDR to the scope that owns it,
following the same slice-order walk tacquito's provider selector uses.
Prints the matching prefix and, if multiple scopes' prefixes cover the
query (e.g. a supernet/subnet overlap), lists the shadowed scopes
below the winner for debugging. An address the scope has tagged with a
vendor
.RB ( "scope devices" )
is shown with its tag.
.SS Per-scope prefixes \fB(tacctl scope prefixes)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "list"
Show the scope's current prefixes.
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " add " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Add one or more CIDRs. Each CIDR is canonicalized
.RB ( 10.1.5.5/24 " -> " 10.1.5.0/24 ),
checked against the one-CIDR-per-scope invariant (rejected with the
owning-scope name if already claimed elsewhere), and appended as its
own
.B secrets[]
entry in the rendered
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
where every entry is sorted globally by prefix
specificity so tacquito's slice-ordered first-match walk routes each
client to the narrowest scope that contains it (and its own RADIUS client).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Remove one or more CIDRs. The last prefix of a scope cannot be removed
(use
.B clear
or
.BR "scope remove" ),
and a prefix whose removal would leave a tagged address
.RB ( "scope devices" )
outside the scope is refused.
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "clear " [ --force ]
Empty the entire prefix list for the scope (with confirmation): the scope
and its per-scope settings are removed, so it vanishes from every rendered
config. The command refuses to proceed when users reference the scope
unless
.B --force
is given, which strips the scope from those users.
.SS Per-scope secret \fB(tacctl scope secret)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "show"
Print the scope's raw shared-secret value plus its length and posture.
The command runs as root, the store is mode 0600, and operators need
the raw value to paste into device configs — so
.B show
prints it rather than hiding it. Superuser only.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "set " value
Set the scope's key after validation (≥
.B config secret-min-length
chars, mixed character classes). With RADIUS enabled, a secret FreeRADIUS
cannot carry (a backslash together with a
.BR $ )
fails the render and is refused;
.B "config render"
warns about one beyond the interoperability advice (63 printable
characters, no space). The secret is shared by TACACS+ and RADIUS.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "generate"
Generate a strong key with
.B openssl rand -base64 24
and apply it.
.SS Per-scope protocols \fB(tacctl scope protocols)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "[list]"
Show which protocols serve the scope. Without a filter, every enabled
backend serves it.
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "set " protocol\fR[,protocol...]
Limit the scope to the named protocols
.RB ( tacacs ", " radius ).
A backend whose protocol is not listed leaves the scope out of what it
renders: for TACACS+ that means its prefixes, its secret and its users'
grant of it are absent from tacquito.yaml; for RADIUS, its prefixes are not
clients. Refused when the list leaves out the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" .
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "clear"
Remove the filter.
.SS Per-scope vendor attributes (RADIUS) \fB(tacctl scope vendor-attrs, tacctl scope devices)\fR
Over RADIUS an Access-Accept always carries
.B Service-Type
.RB ( Administrative-User
at priv-lvl 15, else
.BR NAS-Prompt-User ).
A vendor's privilege attribute is added only where the operator opted in:
.B Cisco-AVPair \(dqshell:priv-lvl=N\(dq
(cisco),
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
(juniper),
.B WTI-Super
0\-3 (wti). An Access-Reject carries none. TACACS+ is not affected.
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope
Show the vendors enabled for the scope, or
.B "not sent"
(the state of a new scope).
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope " enable|disable " vendor\fR[,vendor...]
Enable or disable a vendor's attribute for every device of the scope that
is not tagged. There is no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none :
nothing is sent until it is enabled. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( vendor_attrs ),
so the change re-renders the RADIUS backend and restarts it.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " [list]"
List the scope's tagged addresses.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " set " "ip\fR|\fIcidr vendor"
Tag an address (a bare IP is a /32 or /128) or a range of the scope
with its vendor: it gets that vendor's attribute and no other, whatever
the scope enables. The address must be one
.B "scope lookup"
answers with this scope: inside a prefix of it, and inside no more
specific prefix of another scope. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( devices ).
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " unset " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Remove a tag.
.B "scope prefixes remove"
refuses to leave a tag outside the scope's prefixes (unset it first, or
add the new prefix before removing the old one); tags go with the scope
on rename and remove.
.SS Per-scope idle-session timeout \fB(tacctl scope exec-timeout)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope exec-timeout " scope " [" minutes ]
Show or set the idle-session timeout (in minutes) that
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR
render into device configs. Cisco emits
.BR "exec-timeout \fIn\fR 0"
on
.B line con 0
and
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos emits
.BR "set system login idle-timeout \fIn\fR" .
Range 0..60 (Junos's max). A value of
.B 0
disables idle expiry on both vendors. Default 60.
.SS Per-scope mgmt-acl \fB(tacctl scope mgmt-acl)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " " \
"<list|add|remove|clear|cisco-name|juniper-name>" " [" args "]"
Per-scope management ACL. Subcommand shape mirrors the global
.B "tacctl config mgmt-acl"
so operators who know one form know the other.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR list ,
.BR "add " <cidr>[,...] ,
.BR "remove " <cidr>[,...] ,
.B clear
— manage the per-scope permit list. Render fallback is per-scope
-> global
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits )
-> empty. Empty per-scope lists prune the override so the scope
falls back to the global; `clear` is the explicit way to revert.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "cisco-name " [ label ] ,
.BR "juniper-name " [ label ]
— per-scope ACL / filter name. Fallback is per-scope -> global
.RB ( "config mgmt-acl cisco-name / juniper-name" )
-> shipped default (\fBVTY-ACL\fR for Cisco, \fBMGMT-ACL\fR for
Junos). Name must satisfy ACL-name rules (letter-start +
[A-Za-z0-9_-], 1..63 chars).
.RE
.TP
.IP
Cisco puts the effective name in
.B "ip access-list standard <NAME>"
and the matching
.B "access-class <NAME> in"
on
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos puts it in
.B "set firewall family inet filter <NAME>"
and the commented lo0 apply. Per-scope permits let one environment
deviate without changing the house-wide default.
.SS Per-scope Cisco aaa-group-server label \fB(tacctl scope tacacs-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope tacacs-group " scope " [" name ]
Show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server tacacs+ <NAME>
label for this scope. Rendered into every AAA group definition and
the method-list / accounting lines that reference the group. Per-scope
so each environment can match site naming conventions (e.g.
.BR TACACS_PROD ,
.BR ISE-GROUP ).
Must conform to Cisco ACL naming rules: letter-start, letters, digits,
and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars. Default (unset)
.BR TACACS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent — its AAA configuration does not group named
servers this way.
.SS Per-scope Cisco RADIUS group label \fB(tacctl scope radius-group)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope radius-group " scope " [" name ]
The RADIUS twin of
.BR "scope tacacs-group" :
show or set the Cisco
.B aaa group server radius <NAME>
label rendered by
.BR "config cisco --protocol radius" .
Same naming rules (letter-start, letters, digits and
.BR [_-] ;
1..63 chars). Default (unset)
.BR RADIUS-GROUP .
Junos has no equivalent. Stored as
.B radius_group.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
.SS Per-scope default authentication method \fB(tacctl scope auth-method)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope auth-method " scope " [" tacacs | radius | clear ]
Show or set the protocol this scope uses when a command names none:
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
without
.BR --protocol ,
.B "host enroll"
(for a host that is not registered yet) and
.B "config linux script"
without
.B --method
.RB ( tacacs
is host method
.BR tacplus ).
The order is: an explicit
.B --protocol
or
.BR --method ;
a registered host's own method; this setting; the one protocol the
scope's
.B protocols
filter names, when it names exactly one; the global default (device
configurations TACACS+, hosts
.BR "host default-method" ).
.B clear
removes the setting
.RB ( default
is accepted for it);
.B tacplus
is accepted for
.BR tacacs ,
which is the one spelling it prints.
Refused when the scope's
.B protocols
filter leaves the protocol out;
.B "scope protocols"
in turn refuses a list without the scope's auth-method.
.B "config cisco --legacy"
on a scope that resolves to RADIUS needs
.BR "--protocol tacacs" .
Stored as
.B scope_auth_method.<scope>
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ;
follows
.B "scope rename"
and is removed with the scope.
.SS Per-scope AAA method-list order \fB(tacctl scope aaa-order)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope aaa-order " scope " [" tacacs-first | local-first ]
Show or set the order of methods in the Cisco
.B aaa authentication login
/
.B aaa authorization
lines and Junos
.B system authentication-order
emitted by
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR .
Default (unset)
.B tacacs-first
keeps TACACS+ authoritative (local only kicks in on server outage).
Set
.B local-first
per-scope when a local break-glass account must authenticate even
while TACACS+ is reachable — any local username that collides with a
TACACS+ user will win locally on devices in this scope, so scope
local accounts to emergency / break-glass credentials only.
The change takes effect for the next
.B "config cisco"
/
.B "config juniper"
render of the named scope — existing device configs are unchanged
until the new method-list stanzas are pushed.
.SH RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the configuration for logging in to a device against the RADIUS
backend when given
.BR "--protocol radius" ,
or without the flag for a scope that resolves to RADIUS (its auth-method,
else its only protocol;
.BR "scope auth-method" ).
The server address, the authentication and accounting ports (the
.B auth
and
.B acct
listeners shown by
.BR "config listen --backend radius show" )
and the scope's shared secret are filled in; the management ACL, the
exec timeout, the scope's
.B aaa-order
(the server first, or local first) and the local fallback behave as in
the TACACS+ output. Without the flag, and for a scope that does not
resolve to RADIUS, the output is the TACACS+ configuration, unchanged.
.PP
Cisco output is a
.B "radius server"
block,
.B "aaa group server radius"
(named by
.BR "scope radius-group" ),
.BR "aaa authentication login" ,
.BR "aaa authorization exec"
(the privilege level comes from the reply's
.BR "Cisco-AVPair shell:priv-lvl=N" ,
which the scope must send: see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" )
and
.BR "aaa accounting exec" ,
with the same
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings. Juniper output is
.BR "system radius-server" ,
.BR authentication-order ,
.B "system accounting destination radius"
and the template users that the reply's
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
selects, with the per-class allow-commands and deny-commands rules,
which stay in force because the class is local to the device.
.PP
RADIUS has no per-command authorization and no command accounting, and
tacctl's RADIUS accepts PAP only. Each RADIUS output says under the
configuration what the Access-Accept carries for the device
.RB ( Service-Type
and the vendor's attribute, enabled for the scope or for tagged
addresses only), and which TACACS+ features are not available
(rules set with
.B "group commands"
are not enforced by the server).
.PP
tacctl refuses, with an error and no output, when the configuration
would look right and not work: the RADIUS backend is not enabled
.RB ( "backend enable radius" ),
the scope's
.B "scope protocols"
filter leaves out
.BR radius ,
the scope sends the vendor's attribute to none of its devices (neither
enabled with
.B "scope vendor-attrs"
nor an address tagged with
.BR "scope devices" ;
the error prints the command that enables it),
or the scope secret holds a character that IOS or Junos read as syntax
(whitespace, quotes, a backtick, a non-ASCII character, or any of
.BR "? ! # $ \\e ; { } [ ] | & < > , * ( )" ).
Letters, digits and
.B ". _ + / = : @ % ^ ~ -"
paste unquoted, and
.B "scope secret generate"
makes such a secret. The secret is shared with TACACS+, so changing it
means changing it on every device of the scope. A secret longer than 63
characters renders with a warning. A listener bound to one IPv4 address
supplies the server address; an IPv6 or loopback bind is warned about.
Operator overrides
.IR cisco-radius.template ,
.I juniper-radius.template
and
.I wti-radius.template
in the templates directory work as for the TACACS+ templates; see
.BR FILES .
.SH CONVENTIONS
The following patterns apply uniformly across subcommand families.
.TP
.B No arguments
Dispatcher commands
.RB ( user ,
.BR group ,
.BR config ,
.BR log ,
.BR backup ,
.BR hash ,
and every nested dispatcher such as
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope aaa-order" ,
.BR "scope exec-timeout" ,
.BR "scope tacacs-group" ,
.BR "scope radius-group" ,
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ,
.BR "scope devices" ,
.BR "scope mgmt-acl" ,
.BR "user scope" ,
or
.BR "group privilege" )
print their own usage and return without side effects.
Scalar getter/setters
.RB ( "config loglevel" ,
.BR "config listen" ,
.BR "config metrics" ,
.BR "config password-age" ,
.BR "config bcrypt-cost" ,
.BR "config password-min-length" ,
.BR "config secret-min-length" ,
.BR "config branch" ,
.BR "scope default" )
print the current value when called with no arguments.
.TP
.B Multi-item input
Every
.B add
/
.B remove
subcommand that takes a CIDR or a Cisco exec command accepts either a
single value or a comma-separated list
.RI ( "cidr1,cidr2,..." ).
Every input is validated first; a bad entry aborts the entire
operation without writing anything.
.TP
.B CIDR semantics
Every CIDR-list subcommand
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ,
.BR "config mgmt-acl" )
canonicalizes input before storage:
.B 10.1.5.5/24
becomes
.BR 10.1.5.0/24 ;
.B 2001:DB8::/32
becomes
.BR 2001:db8::/32 .
Exact duplicates (after canonicalization) are rejected as no-ops on
.BR add .
Overlapping CIDRs of different prefix lengths coexist
.RI ( "10.0.0.0/8 and 10.99.0.0/16 can both be present" ).
Stored order is by broadcast-address ascending (IPv4 before IPv6):
disjoint ranges sort by their end address, and an overlapping subnet
falls immediately above its containing supernet (the subnet's range
ends before the supernet's). This groups related CIDRs together and
gives tacquito's provider selector the "most-specific first among
overlaps" ordering it needs so that a narrower scope wins a first-
match lookup over a broader scope that contains it.
.TP
.B Scope prefix invariants
Every CIDR (after canonicalization) belongs to
.B exactly one
scope. Adding a prefix already owned by a different scope is rejected
with a message naming the owning scope; the operator must remove it
from the owning scope first with
.B "scope prefixes " \fIowner\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR
before re-adding it elsewhere. Overlapping prefixes
.I across
scopes are allowed and routed correctly — a scope with
.B 10.5.0.0/16
coexists with a scope holding the supernet
.BR 10.0.0.0/8 .
.PP
To make first-match-wins honor cross-scope specificity, tacctl emits
one
.B secrets:
entry per (scope, prefix) pair — a scope with N prefixes becomes N
entries that share
.BR name: " and " secret.key: .
Entries are re-sorted globally by prefix specificity
(v4 before v6; smaller broadcast first) after every mutation, so
tacquito's slice-ordered provider walk routes each client IP to the
narrowest scope that contains it. The CLI continues to present the
logical one-scope-per-bundle view via
.BR "scope list" ", " "scope show" ", and " "config show" .
.PP
Do not hand-edit the
.B secrets:
block under multi-prefix scopes — the same-name entries must carry
identical
.B secret.key
values.
.B config validate
reports any divergence.
.TP
.B "clear"
.B clear
subcommands always prompt with
.B [y/N]
and print a warning about the resulting posture (for example, "no
clients can connect" or "fails open").
.TP
.B "Filters and opt-ins"
A setting that narrows something down is a filter: its verbs are
.B set
and
.BR clear ,
and empty (cleared) means everything
.RB ( "scope protocols" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ).
A setting that turns something on is an opt-in: its verbs are
.B enable
and
.BR disable ,
and nothing is on until it is enabled
.RB ( "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
An opt-in has no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none ,
so that nothing in it can read as "empty means all".
.TP
.B Service restart
Changes to the store, and to the
.I tacctl.yaml
settings a backend renders (command rules, listeners,
.BR backends.* ),
re-render every enabled backend and restart the backends whose files
changed. Settings only device configurations read (mgmt-acl permits and
names, per-group priv-exec mappings, per-scope AAA order, exec timeout and
group labels) and tacctl's own tunables (bcrypt cost, password age, \&...)
restart nothing.
.TP
.B Prompts
Every confirmation prompt cancels, with a message, when standard input is
closed.
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes ,
.B \-\-force
where documented) answers yes.
.TP
.B Flags
Long-form flags
.RI ( --hash ,
.IR --scopes ,
.IR --scope ,
.IR --prefixes ,
.IR --secret ,
.IR --protocols ,
.IR --vendor-attrs ,
.IR --match ,
.IR --action ,
.IR --branch ,
.IR --protocol ,
.IR --method ,
.IR --backend ,
.IR --listener ,
.IR --server ,
.IR --name ,
.IR --port ,
.IR --identity ,
.IR --output ,
.IR --adopt )
take a single argument;
.IR --default ,
.IR --force ,
.IR --legacy ,
.IR --check ,
.IR --replace ,
.IR --json ,
.IR --local ,
.IR --all ,
.IR --build-on-host ,
.I --allow-uid-mismatch
and
.I -y
take none. Required positional arguments come before flags.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B tacacs
TACACS+, served by tacquito built from source; installed by
.B "tacctl install"
and enabled by default.
.TP
.B radius
RADIUS, served by FreeRADIUS from the distribution's package; installed and
enabled by
.BR "tacctl backend enable radius" .
.PP
Every change renders the configuration of every enabled backend from the
store, all or none, and restarts only the backends whose files changed.
With more than one backend enabled,
.BR status ,
.BR "config show" ,
.B "config validate"
and
.B log
print a labelled section per backend
.RB ( "== Backend: radius (radius, freeradius) ==" ).
.PP
.B Listeners
are
.BI listeners. backend . name
in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "config listen" ).
The TACACS+ listener
.B default
(tcp :49) runs in
.BR tacquito.service ;
every further one is an instance
.BI tacquito@ name .service
of
.BR tacquito@.service ,
.B PartOf
and
.B WantedBy
.BR tacquito.service ,
so
.B "systemctl restart tacquito"
covers them all. Each unit has a rendered drop-in
.IR <unit>.d/tacctl.conf .
RADIUS listeners
.RB ( auth ,
udp :1812;
.BR acct ,
udp :1813) are
.B listen
sections of tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration. TACACS+ over TLS is reserved
in the schema and not implemented.
.PP
.B RADIUS.
.B "backend enable radius"
installs
.B freeradius
and
.B freeradius\-utils
(Debian/Ubuntu; RHEL family from AppStream) and starts the distribution's
unit
.RB ( freeradius
or
.BR radiusd )
with a drop-in that runs tacctl's own instance
.RB ( "\-n tacctl\-radius \-D" " <its dictionary>" ):
the package's configuration is not read, edited or unlinked, so there is no
EAP, inner tunnel, proxying or default client. A unit that is already
running or enabled without tacctl's drop-in is somebody's RADIUS server and
is refused. One client per prefix of every scope RADIUS serves, with the
scope's secret; a user authenticates only from a client in one of their
scopes; disabled users and the
.B root
sink never do. PAP only, against the stored bcrypt hashes (no CHAP,
MS-CHAP or EAP). No per-command authorization and no command accounting.
An Access-Accept carries
.B Service-Type
always, and a vendor's privilege attribute only where the scope opts in
.RB ( "scope vendor-attrs" ", " "scope devices" ).
FreeRADIUS 3.2.5 and 3.0.27 log a "BlastRADIUS check" block for a client the
first time it sends Message-Authenticator and require it from that client
from then on. Verified against FreeRADIUS 3.0.20, 3.0.27 and 3.2.5;
nothing was tested on Cisco, Juniper or WTI hardware.
.SH STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
(users, groups, scopes, filters) and
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
(settings) are canonical. Generated from them:
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
and the tacquito unit drop-ins; for RADIUS
.IR tacctl\-radius.conf ,
.I tacctl\-radius.users
and
.I tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
in the FreeRADIUS configuration directory.
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
holds their checksums.
.PP
A change is checked against hand edits, snapshotted, written, rendered into
a staging area for every enabled backend and proved there (read back, the
daemon's own config check), then installed, and the backends whose files
changed are restarted; a failure at any step puts the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and every generated file back.
.PP
.B Drift.
A generated file that no longer matches its checksum was edited by hand:
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
show a
.B DRIFT
line and every change is refused (exit 3). Keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
with
.B "store import \-\-replace"
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" ;
discard it with
.B "config render \-\-force"
(a copy goes to
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ ).
RADIUS files can only be re-rendered.
.PP
.B Legacy read-only mode.
Without a store (an upgrade whose gate stopped, or after
.BR "store rollback" )
tacctl reads
.I tacquito.yaml
directly: read commands work, commands that change users, groups, scopes
or filters are refused with
.IR "store not initialised" .
Only TACACS+ exists in that mode.
.SH UPGRADING TO 0.1.15
The first
.B "tacctl upgrade"
from 0.1.14 or earlier pulls 0.1.15, re-executes it, and then:
.IP 1. 4
moves
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups/
and
.I templates/
from
.I /etc/tacquito
to
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
leaving symlinks at the old paths for one release (idempotent; a regular
file written there by older code wins over the moved copy, which is kept
under
.IR backups/legacy/ );
.IP 2. 4
installs
.B tacquito.service
and
.BR tacquito@.service ,
imports the hand-managed drop-in
.I tacctl\-overrides.conf
into
.I tacctl.yaml
once, renders
.I tacctl.conf
in its place and keeps the old file under
.IR backups/legacy/ ;
.IP 3. 4
runs the store gate:
.B "store import \-\-check"
(import, render, equivalence of what tacquito loads from both files, a load
test of the rendered file with the new binary on a loopback port). Only when
it passes is the store written, the old file kept as
.IR backups/legacy/tacquito.yaml.pre\-store. <timestamp>,
.I tacquito.yaml
rendered and tacquito restarted.
.PP
When the gate stops (unrepresentable content, a render that is not
equivalent, the binary or
.B timeout
missing) the upgrade still completes, leaves
.I tacquito.yaml
and the daemon as they were, and tacctl is in legacy read-only mode. It never
forces either through. Fix what
.B "store import \-\-check"
reports and upgrade again, or accept the difference:
.B "store import"
.RB [ \-\-force ],
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" .
.B "store rollback"
returns to the pre-store file; run it before checking out an older release,
which edits
.I tacquito.yaml
directly.
.SH FILES
.SS tacctl
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/
State directory, mode 0700, root only.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
The canonical users, groups, scopes and connection filters (shared secrets and
password hashes included). Mode 0600, root only. Every command that changes
them renders each enabled backend's config from it. Print it with
.BR "tacctl store show" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
Operator overrides for tacctl tunables and settings (bcrypt cost, password /
secret minimum lengths, password age warning, default scope, default host
method, mgmt-acl permits + names, per-group command rules and Cisco
priv-exec mappings, per-scope device-config settings, listeners,
.BR backends.* ).
Canonical defaults are embedded in
.I lib/conf.sh
and emitted on demand by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" .
Only set keys you want to change \(em missing keys inherit from the
defaults, and setting a key back to the default value removes it. An
empty overrides file is deleted. Every write is schema-validated
(out-of-range numbers, bad ACL names, malformed CIDRs, colliding listeners,
typo'd keys are rejected before anything is written). Inspect the merged
view with
.BR "tacctl config dump" ;
re-check the file with
.BR "tacctl config validate" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
The checksum of every file tacctl rendered, from which
.B status
and
.B config validate
report a hand edit as drift.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/
Automatic snapshots of the canonical files, one directory per snapshot, last
30 retained, root-only;
.I legacy/
holds old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
backups from earlier releases, the pre-store config and files a render or
the state migration displaced.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
Host-specific overrides for the device config templates
.RI ( cisco ,
.IR cisco-legacy ,
.IR juniper ,
.IR wti ,
and for
.BR "--protocol radius"
.IR cisco-radius ,
.IR juniper-radius " and " wti-radius ).
A file named
.I <name>.template
here is used in place of the one shipped in
.IR config/templates/ .
.TP
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-hosts ", " /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids
The registry of enrolled Linux hosts (with each host's method) and the
UID/GID each user gets on every host.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/linux/
The pam_tacplus source tarball and the modules built in containers.
.TP
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl ", " /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
Optional sudoers rules. Installed only via
.B "tacctl config sudoers install"
and
.BR "tacctl config sudoers tiers install" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
Symlink to
.IR /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh .
.TP
.I /opt/tacctl/
Clone of the management (tacctl) repository. Source of the symlinked
CLI and the source directory consulted by
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.SS TACACS+ (tacquito)
.TP
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
TACACS+ server configuration, rendered by tacctl from the store. Mode 0640,
owned by
.BR tacquito : tacquito .
.I /etc/tacquito
also holds a copy of the README and, for one release, symlinks from the old
paths of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups
and
.I templates
into
.IR /etc/tacctl .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service
Systemd unit file (the default listener).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito@.service
Template unit, one instance per further listener.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service.d/tacctl.conf
Systemd drop-in holding the listen address, log level, metrics address
and accounting log of the unit. Rendered by tacctl from
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "tacctl config listen" ,
.BR "tacctl config loglevel" ,
.BR "tacctl config metrics" );
edits are overwritten. Instances have
.IR tacquito@<name>.service.d/tacctl.conf .
.TP
.I /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log
Per-session accounting records
.RI ( accounting\-<name>.log
per further listener). Rotated daily, retained 90 days
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacquito ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito
The tacquito server binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito-hashgen
Standalone bcrypt hash generator shipped with tacquito.
.TP
.I /opt/tacquito-src/
Clone of the tacquito server source code, used to rebuild the binary
during
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.SS RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.I <raddb>
is
.I /etc/freeradius/3.0
(Debian/Ubuntu) or
.I /etc/raddb
(RHEL family);
.I <logs>
is
.I /var/log/freeradius
or
.IR /var/log/radius .
No file of the FreeRADIUS package is edited.
.TP
.IR <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.conf ", " <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.users
tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration (listeners, clients, policy) and its users
file (bcrypt hashes), rendered from the store; mode 0640, root and the
daemon's group.
.TP
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
The dictionary the daemon is started with: the package's, then the WTI
vendor and tacctl's internal attributes.
.TP
.IR /etc/systemd/system/freeradius.service.d/tacctl.conf " (" radiusd.service.d " on RHEL)"
Drop-in that starts the distribution's unit as tacctl's instance; present
exactly while the backend is enabled.
.TP
.IR <logs>/tacctl\-auth.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-accounting.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-radius.log
One line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject; accounting detail records; the
daemon's log
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacctl\-radius ).
.SS Linux hosts
.TP
.IR /etc/pam.d/tacctl\-* ", " /etc/tacctl\-pam_radius.conf ", " /var/lib/tacctl\-client/
On an enrolled host: the PAM files tacctl writes (root-only; for tacplus
they hold the shared secret), the pam_radius_auth server file (0600), and
the client's state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install on a fresh host:
.B tacctl install
.TP
Add a superuser:
.B tacctl user add alice superuser
.TP
Restrict device subnets in the default scope:
.B tacctl scope prefixes lab add 10.1.0.0/24,172.16.5.0/24
.TP
Build a multi-environment scope hierarchy (broader prod scopes with
narrower lab scopes carved out — the /24 wins first-match for 10.10.99.x
clients, the /16 catches the rest of 10.10.x):
.nf
.B "  tacctl scope add prod_dc_east --prefixes 10.10.0.0/16 --secret generate"
.B "  tacctl scope add lab_east     --prefixes 10.10.99.0/24 --secret generate"
.fi
.TP
Trace which scope a client IP falls into:
.B tacctl scope lookup 10.10.99.7
.TP
Grant a user full production access across two datacenters:
.B tacctl user scope alice add prod_dc_east,prod_dc_west
.TP
Generate a Cisco-ready configuration for the prod scope:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east
.TP
Generate legacy IOS 12.x syntax for an older device (e.g. an ITP on 12.4):
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --legacy
.TP
Print the serial-menu walkthrough for a WTI console server in the lab scope:
.B tacctl config wti --scope lab_east
.TP
Serve the same users over RADIUS, with Cisco and Juniper privilege attributes for prod:
.nf
.B "  tacctl backend enable radius"
.B "  tacctl scope vendor-attrs prod_dc_east enable cisco,juniper"
.B "  tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --protocol radius"
.fi
.TP
Tag one address of a scope as a WTI unit (it gets WTI-Super and nothing else):
.B tacctl scope devices lab_east set 10.10.99.5 wti
.TP
Enroll a Linux host with pam_radius_auth, then switch it to pam_tacplus:
.nf
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method radius"
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method tacplus"
.fi
.TP
Bind the listeners to a specific management IP:
.nf
.B "  tacctl config listen tcp 10.1.0.1:49"
.B "  tacctl config listen --backend radius --listener auth udp 10.1.0.1:1812"
.fi
.TP
Check, without writing anything, whether a tacquito.yaml can move into the store:
.B tacctl store import --check
.TP
Expose the Prometheus exporter to an external scraper (default is loopback-only):
.B tacctl config metrics address 10.1.0.1:8080
.TP
Enable passwordless invocation for members of the adm group:
.B tacctl config sudoers install adm
.TP
Print client-side bcrypt generation instructions to share with a user:
.B tacctl hash commands
.SH NOTES
.B Unified tacctl config.
Every tacctl tunable \(em bcrypt cost, password / secret minimum lengths,
password-age warning threshold, default scope, mgmt-acl permits + ACL names,
and per-group Cisco priv-exec mappings \(em is driven by two sources:
the canonical defaults embedded in
.I lib/conf.sh
(printed by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" )
and operator overrides in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Missing keys inherit from the defaults; setting a key back to the default
value removes it; an empty overrides file is pruned. Writes are
schema-validated (bad ranges, typo'd keys, malformed CIDRs rejected
before anything lands on disk).
.PP
.B IPv4 only by default.
The TACACS+ listener runs with
.B -network tcp
and the RADIUS ones with
.BR udp ,
which bind IPv4 only. Switching to
.B tcp6
or
.B udp6
enables a dual-stack socket on most platforms; IPv4 clients then
connect with IPv4-mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:10.1.0.1 )
that do not match IPv4 prefix ACLs configured via
.BR "tacctl scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
or
.BR "config deny" .
Add equivalent IPv6 prefix rules before enabling
.B tcp6.
.PP
.B Juniper template users are required.
Every
.B local-user-name
referenced by a group class (for example
.BR RO-CLASS ,
.BR OP-CLASS ,
.BR RW-CLASS )
must have a matching local user on each Juniper device. Without this,
authentication succeeds but Junos rejects the login silently.
Run
.B tacctl config juniper
to regenerate the device-side configuration after adding or editing
groups.
.PP
.B Config edits.
.B tacctl
creates a snapshot before every change and restarts what the change
affects. Generated files
.RI ( tacquito.yaml ,
the unit drop-ins, the RADIUS files) are overwritten; a hand edit of one is
drift and blocks every change until it is adopted or discarded (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ).
